  "body": "# Gaoxy 0.13.0\n\nNative WebSock connections default to persistent connections without frame-size,\nmessage-size, fragment-count, callback-batch-count, inactivity or absolute-lifetime\nceilings. Explicit application limits remain supported. Generic service byte and\nstate budgets no longer cap retained WebSocket messages or callback state;\ntransport staging, queues and protocol windows stay finite and reusable.\n\nThe WebSock API still delivers complete messages, so memory grows with the\nmessage, application state and pending output. Applications that produce data\nindependently need their own production policy. Protocol validation and mandatory\ncontrol-frame limits remain enforced. Callback execution, bounded writes and\nnormal close retain separate finite deadlines; an operation deadline does not\ncover an entire progressing message or output batch.\n\nPhoenix applications must explicitly configure\n`websocket: [timeout: :infinity]` on their socket declarations to obtain\npersistent connections. Authentication, Origin checks, heartbeats and deliberate\napplication close policies remain under application control. The separately\nconfigured message-level `Gaoxy.Proxy.WebSocket` bridge retains its own policies.\nSee the [capacity and configuration guide](../websocket-limits.md).\n\nThe H1/H2/H3 persistence goal uses infinite H1 reads after upgrade, H2 owner idle\nsuspension while WebSockets exist with restoration after the final WebSocket,\nand H3 local idle suspension with keepalive below a finite peer idle period.\nHell 0.1.3 supplies the released public `Hell.Handler.set_read_timeout/2` API\nrequired for H2. Focused regressions verify quiet persistence, repeated exact\ntransfers, HTTP siblings, final-owner idle restoration and preservation of finite\npartial-frame and explicit application lifetime deadlines.\n\nHTTP/2 startup now treats a confirmed peer closure as a normal stop when setting\nTCP/TLS receive options races with disconnect. Bare and SSL-wrapped failures\nrequire confirmation through the supported socket API; live-peer option failures\nretain their original error disposition. See the\n[issue #17 acceptance record](../acceptance/internal-17-startup-peer-close.md).\n\nThe dependency cohort includes Hell 0.1.3, Abyss 0.10.0 and the HTTP/TLS/QUIC\npackages at 0.21.17. The direct `http_core` requirement now has a 0.21.17 floor.\nPlug.Crypto advances to 2.2.1. All six Hex distributions and the standalone\napplication use version `0.13.0`.\n\nHistorical failures and specialist abnormal, slow-peer, pressure and\nsustained-load qualification keep their recorded dispositions. See the\n[issue #16 qualification](../acceptance/internal-16-default-capacity.md).\n",
